1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,300
Every hackathon starts with a skeleton. SkeleKit asks: what if the skeleton IS the product?

2
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,200
One template — two apps as different as they get: a canvas arcade game, and a deadline dashboard.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:20,300
Grave Dash: a one-button arcade game — physics, parallax, best-score persistence.

4
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:29,100
The best score persists through the skeleton's typed store.

5
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,000
OppBoard: real deadlines, D-day counts, pinning — same bones, zero core edits.

6
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:44,900
It starts with steering docs — and one hard rule: apps may override theme.css only. Never core/.

7
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,700
Each app is a three-file spec pack: requirements, design, tasks. The only thing written by hand.

8
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:58,800
Kiro executed every task — the flipped markers are the agent's own breadcrumbs.

9
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:08,800
The proof: after both apps were built, the core modules are byte-identical. Zero drift.

10
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:17,800
27 core tests, 43 more in each app — all green, strict TypeScript, zero runtime deps.

11
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,600
The skeleton's fixed-timestep loop gives the game deterministic physics…

12
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:36,300
…while OppBoard never boots the loop at all. Filters write to the hash — the URL is a shareable, pre-filtered view.

13
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:43,000
Pins survive refresh through the typed store — the same module behind the game's best score.

14
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:50,000
One skeleton. Two very different bodies. Copy the template, write a spec pack, let Kiro spawn your app.
